  [1mThe fork:[0m you want [38;5;153muserId[39m as a real input to [38;5;153mlogin[39m, but something has to produce that value before [38;5;153mlogin[39m is called —
  and which thing it is decides the persistence mechanism, whether [38;5;153mindex.html[39m grows a field, and whether this is an
  identity or an analytics concern.

  - [1mApp-generated correlation id[0m — the app mints an id on first visit, stores it, and passes the same one to [38;5;153mlogin[39m and
    later to other forms. Buys tracking that works across forms immediately and needs no server. Costs: it identifies a
    [3mbrowser[0m, not a person, so "who logged in" is only answerable once you join it to the username server-side.
  - [1mUser-supplied id[0m — an account number or tenant id typed into the login form. Buys a real business identifier. Costs:
    a new field in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, validation in [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m, and users have to know their id. Hard to undo once people
    are trained on the field.
  - [1mExternally provided id[0m — from the URL ([38;5;153m?uid=[39m), an SSO token, or a value your backend already sets in a cookie. Buys
    a genuine identity with no UI change. Costs: depends on infrastructure I can't see from this repo, and the shape is
    dictated by that system.

  [1mMy recommendation is the app-generated correlation id[0m, because it's the only one of the three this repo can implement
  end-to-end today, and it's the shape that actually matches "persists and works across the app, other forms will need
  it later." The other two make [38;5;153muserId[39m a per-form input rather than ambient state.

  One thing I'll want to settle right after this, not now: if the id turns out to be a real account identifier rather
  than an anonymous one, persisting it in browser storage has consequences worth a deliberate decision — it survives
  logout on shared machines and is readable by any script on the origin. I'll raise it once I know which kind of id
  we're dealing with.
